{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Remember the Sabbath day.</b> Since it gives a reason for remembering the Sabbath more than the other commandments, as it is written: ״כי ששת ימים״ etc. — for all the commandments a person is obligated to observe them by reasoning alone, except for the Sabbath — the expression of remembrance, which does not serve for the future but only for the past, applies to it. And in Deuteronomy, where it gives a reason for keeping the Sabbath — ״למען ינוח״ etc. — the expression of guarding applies to it. Another interpretation: <b>Remember the Sabbath day</b> — which I gave you at Marah; therefore the expression of remembrance applies to it. Another interpretation: <b>Remember the Sabbath day</b> — every day, remember which day of the week it is, that you were commanded <b>to sanctify it</b>, and all this is so that you may keep the seventh day. And the expression of remembrance includes guarding as well, for when 'remember' was said, all who heard understood that its meaning is like 'keep,' as if they were said simultaneously. Here Rashi explained: ״זכור״ and ״שמור״ were spoken in a single utterance, etc. And if you ask: how do 'remember' and 'keep' resemble ״מחלליה מות יומת״ and ״וביום השבת שני כבשים״? One may answer: 'remember' is a positive commandment, and 'keep' is a negative commandment, as our Rabbis taught everywhere: wherever it says השמר, פן, or אל, it is nothing but a negative commandment. And it comes to say that women are obligated in the sanctification of the day by Torah law, since whoever is included in 'keep' is included in 'remember.'"], :book :exodus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:59:03.944919274Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}